two T6 and two T7 \u2014 we have only one T6.\n\nNot enough.\n\nAlternative: **All Pungs**? No.\n\nBut note: we have a kong and a chow \u2014 both in different suits.\n\nBut we can aim for **Half Flush** (Bamboos and honors) \u2014 but we have no honors.\n\nOr **Outside Hand** \u2014 but B1 is terminal, T4 is not.\n\nT4 is simple.\n\nBetter: **All Simples** (2 points) \u2014 requires no terminals (1 or 9) and no honors.\n\nCurrently, B1 is terminal \u2014 so if we discard B1, and win with all simples, we get All Simples.\n\nAlso, we have no honors \u2014 so **No Honors** (1 point).\n\nWe have a kong \u2014 exposed \u2014 so **Melded Kong** (1 point).\n\nWe have multiple pungs? Only one kong.\n\nOther patterns: **Two Concealed Pungs**? No \u2014 both melds exposed.\n\n**Fully Concealed Hand**? No \u2014 we have exposed melds.\n\nBut we might win by self-draw or claim.\n\nNow, **Seat Wind is North (F4)**, Prevailing Wind is North.\n\nWe do not have F4 in hand, and no pung \u2014 so no Seat Wind or Prevalent Wind pattern.\n\nIs **All Chows** possible? We have one chow, one kong \u2014 kong disqualifies All Chows.\n\nSo not.\n\nBest chance: reduce risk and aim for a simple wait.\n\nNow, assess danger of each discard:\n\n- **B1**: Terminal. Has been discarded by us and previous player. Others may be avoiding it. B1-B2-B3 possible, but B2 unshown:3, B3:4 \u2014 available. But no one has shown interest. Probably safe. And discarding it removes terminal, helping toward All Simples.\n\n- **B5**: Part of B5-B6-B7 \u2014 we already have that chow. Another B5 \u2014 could be used in 4-5-6 or 5-6-7. B4 is scarce (unshown:1), B6:2 left \u2014 possible. Slight risk.\n\n- **B6**: Same \u2014 B5-B6-B7. B7 is in our meld, but others could use. Across player has W6-W7-W8, not Bamboos. Next player has B8 pung \u2014 might be going for lower Dots? Possible B6-B7-B8. B7 unshown:2 \u2014 available. So B6 could complete B6-B7-B8. **Dangerous**.\n\n- **T5**: Could be used in T3-T4-T5 (T3:3 