* (24 points)!\n\nAh! Critical insight.\n\nIf we discard B1, and our hand is all Bamboos, and we win, we can get **Full Flush** (24 points) \u2014 easily meets 8-fan minimum.\n\nYes! Full Flush: hand consisting of only suited tiles of the same suit.\n\nEven with mixed suits in melds? No \u2014 **Full Flush** requires *all* tiles in the hand to be of the same suit.\n\nBut our melds: [B5,B6,B7] is Dots, [T4\u00d74] is Bamboos \u2014 **different suits**.\n\nOh! Problem.\n\nWe have a meld in Dots (B5-B6-B7) and a kong in Bamboos (T4\u00d74), and hand has T5,T6,T7,T8 (Bamboos) and B1 (Dots).\n\nSo we have **two suits** \u2014 cannot make Full Flush.\n\nEven if we discard B1, we still have Dots and Bamboos \u2014 so **not pure**.\n\nSo **Full Flush** impossible.\n\nSimilarly, **Half Flush** requires one suit + honors \u2014 we have two suits, no honors \u2014 doesn't qualify.\n\nSo best we can hope for is **All Simples** (if we drop B1), **No Honors**, **Melded Kong**, and perhaps **All Pungs**? No \u2014 we have a chow.\n\nPatterns:\n- We have a kong and a chow \u2014 so not eligible for All Pungs, All Chows, etc.\n\nBut we might have **Mixed Triple Chow** or similar \u2014 unlikely.\n\nAnother idea: **Upper Tiles**? Upper Tiles: only 7,8,9. We have T5,T6 \u2014 not upper.\n\n**Lower Four**? 1,2,3,4 \u2014 we have T4,T5 \u2014 T5 is not lower four.\n\nNo clear high-point pattern.\n\nBut we can aim for minimal 8 fans.\n\nCurrently, possible:\n- All Pungs? No \u2014 we have a chow.\n- Half Flush? No \u2014 two suits.\n- Outside Hand? Requires every meld to have terminal or honor \u2014 our [B5,B6,B7] is all simples \u2014 not outside.\n\nSo hard to reach 8.\n\nBut we have **Three Kongs**? No \u2014 only one.\n\nWait \u2014 we have only one kong.\n\nBut let's count what we can score:\n\nSuppose we win with current structure:\n- Meld 1: [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 chow, mixed\n- Meld 2: [T4\u00d74] \u2014 kong, exposed\n- Two more melds and a pair from hand.\n\nBut hand has T5,T6,T7,T7,T8,B1 \u2014 can form T5-T6-T7, and T7-T8-T9? No T9.\n\nOr T6-T7-T8, and pair T7.\n\nBut need another meld.\n\nUnless we have more tiles.\n\nPerhaps we are close to T5-T6-T7 and T7-T8-T9.\n\nBut T9 is scarce.\n\nBetter: **Short Straight** (2 points): two chows in same suit with successive numbers, e.g., 2-3-4 and 5-6-7.\n\nWe have T4 kong \u2014 so T4 is not in chow.\n\nBut if we had T5-T6-T7 and T6-T7-T8, that would be Short Straight.\n\nWe have T5,T6,T7,T7,T8 \u2014 so if we form T5-T6-T7 and T6-T7-T8, that's overlapping, but possible with five tiles.\n\nThen pair? We have only six tiles \u2014 after two chows (6 tiles), need pair \u2014 but no duplicate.\n\nUnless pair of T7 \u2014 but using two T7s.\n\nSo hand: T5,T6,T7,T7,T8,B1\n\nCan form:\n- T5-T6-T7\n- T7-T8-T9? No T9\n- T6-T7-T8 \u2014 uses T6,T7,T8\n- Leaves T5,T7,B1 \u2014 no pair\n\nOr:\n- T5-T6-T7 (using one T7)\n- T6-T7-T8 \u2014 need another T6 and T7 \u2014 only one T6\n\nImpossible.\n\nBest is one chow and a pair.\n\nFor example: T5-T6-T7, and pair T7, and pair B1? But only one B1.\n\nSo likely: win with T7 pair, and chows including T5-T6-T7 and something else.\n\nBut we already have two melds \u2014 need two more.\n\nWith five tiles, hard.\n\nPerhaps we are one tile away from completing a meld.\n\nBut focus on discard safety.\n\nRe-evaluate danger:\n\n- **B1**: Low demand. Discarded by us and previous player. B2, B3 available, but no one is collecting low Dots. Safe.\n\n- **B5/B6**: Part of B5-B6-B7 \u2014 we have that meld, but others could use. B6 especially \u2014 next player has B8 pung, might want B6-B7-B8. B7 available (unshown:2), so **B6 is dangerous**.\n\n- **T5**: Can only be used in T5-T6-T7. T6:2 left, T7:3 left \u2014 very live. Dangerous.\n\n- **T6**: Same \u2014 T5-T6-T7 or T6-T7-T8. Very live.\n\n- **T7**: T6-T7-T8 or T7-T8-T9. T8:2 left, T9:1 left \u2014 T7-T8-T9 possible. But T9 is rare. Still, T7 is highly usable.\n\n- **T8**: T6-T7-T8 or T7-T8-T9. T6 and T7 available. But next player discarded T8 twice \u2014 strong signal it's safe.\n\nIn mahjong, when a player discards a tile twice early, it's often safe.\n\nNext player discarded T8 twice \u2014 so likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, across and previous haven't shown interest.\n\nSo **T8** is relatively safe.\n\n**B1** is also safe.\n\nBut between B1 and T8, which to prefer?\n\nDiscarding B1 removes a terminal, improving chance for **All Simples** (2 points).\n\nDiscarding T8 keeps T5,T6,T7,T7 \u2014 better for forming T5-T6-T7 and pair T7.\n\nAnd T8 is safer than T5,T6,T7.\n\nSo **Play B1** is best.\n\nIt improves hand value and is relatively safe.\n\nReason: B1 is a terminal tile that limits the potential for scoring patterns like All Simples. Discarding it increases the chance of achieving All Simples (2 points) and No Honors (1 point) upon winning. While B1 is not completely safe, it is less likely to be needed by opponents compared to central tiles like T5, T6, T7, or B6, which can complete common chows. Among the safe discards, B1 provides the greatest strategic improvement to the hand's scoring potential.\n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
